WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.700
A chain of a hundred residues looks as if it could bend any way at all.

2
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:09.560
Can it?

3
00:00:09.783 --> 00:00:13.403
The answer starts with the bond that joins one residue to the next.

4
00:00:14.223 --> 00:00:15.363
It is the peptide bond.

5
00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:21.667
It joins the carbon of one residue's C=O group to the nitrogen of the next residue's

6
00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:22.667
N–H group.

7
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.420
Chemists can draw this bond two ways.

8
00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.770
In one drawing, carbon and oxygen share a double bond.

9
00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.650
The bond from carbon to nitrogen is single.

10
00:00:33.683 --> 00:00:36.563
In the other, oxygen carries a negative charge.

11
00:00:37.723 --> 00:00:41.903
Carbon and nitrogen share a double bond, and nitrogen carries a positive charge.

12
00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:45.033
The real bond is a blend of both.

13
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:48.653
So the bond from carbon to nitrogen is partly double.

14
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:53.063
That makes it short, about 1.32 Å.

15
00:00:53.917 --> 00:00:57.097
A double bond cannot twist without losing its overlap.

16
00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:59.657
So the peptide bond does not rotate.

17
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.960
Six atoms are locked in one flat plane.

18
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.300
The Cα of the first residue.

19
00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.980
Its C and O. The N and H of the next residue.

20
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.460
And that residue's Cα.

21
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:19.773
Almost always, the two alpha carbons sit on opposite sides of the bond.

22
00:01:20.573 --> 00:01:21.613
That is the trans form.

23
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:25.717
The cis form, with both on the same side, is rare.

24
00:01:26.637 --> 00:01:29.277
It is more common when the next residue is proline.

25
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.600
So picture the backbone as a string of flat, rigid cards.

26
00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.020
One card for each peptide bond.

27
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.180
The cards are joined at each Cα.

28
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.080
Watch what can move.

29
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.240
Each residue has three backbone bonds.

30
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.460
The peptide bond is frozen.

31
00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.460
Only two can turn, one on each side of the Cα.

32
00:01:51.483 --> 00:01:52.863
Watch one hinge turn.

33
00:01:53.743 --> 00:01:57.403
It is the rotation about the bond from nitrogen to the Cα.

34
00:01:58.223 --> 00:01:59.223
It is called phi.

35
00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:05.927
Now the other. It is the rotation about the bond from the Cα to the next carbon.

36
00:02:05.927 --> 00:02:07.647
It is called psi.

37
00:02:08.617 --> 00:02:11.537
Two angles per residue, repeated down the chain.

38
00:02:12.637 --> 00:02:14.497
Together they set the path of the whole backbone.

39
00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:17.507
Two free angles sound like freedom.

40
00:02:18.527 --> 00:02:20.487
Next, we see how much freedom they really give.

41
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.580
The peptide bond is flat and rigid.

42
00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.240
Each residue turns on just two hinges.
